+### Across the fleet, and accepting a vulnerability
+
+**Security → Container vulnerabilities** (`/security/containers`,
+`machine.view`, scoped to the machines the account can see) lists every
+critical and high vulnerability in the stored scans: how many machines
+and images have it, whether a fixed version exists, most severe and
+widespread first (searchable by id or package, at most 500 rows). Each
+vulnerability's page shows every machine, image, package, installed and
+fixed version - "which of my machines run an image with CVE-...?" in one
+place. The critical and high findings are kept one per row
+(`docker_image_findings`, indexed by vulnerability id) for this; the
+upgrade to 0.125.0 fills it from the scans already stored.
+
+A critical or high vulnerability can be **accepted** until a date
+(`action.updates`, and only by an account not limited to some machine
+groups, since it applies everywhere): for every image, or for one image
+repository (`docker.io/library/nginx`, all its tags), with a reason, for
+1-365 days (90 by default). Until then it is left out of each scan's
+counts (what the scanners found stays in `raw_counts`), so the gate, the
+machine's totals, the vulnerability conditions and every count on the
+Docker tab no longer see it; the image's vulnerability list marks it
+*accepted until ...*. The counts follow within a minute (a background
+job), and an hourly job removes acceptances past their date so the
+vulnerability counts again. Revoking does the same at once
+(`app/services/docker_acceptances.py`).
+
 ### Clean-up (prune)
 
 The Images page's **Clean up** (`action.updates`) shows how many stopped
@@ -629,7 +684,7 @@ without a confirmed host key or without Docker access are skipped:
 
 ## Notifications
 
-Five events ([Notifications](Notifications#events)) and three condition
+Nine events ([Notifications](Notifications#events)) and three condition
 fields:
 
 | Event | Fires when | Placeholders |
@@ -639,6 +694,21 @@ fields:
 | `docker.update.blocked` | the vulnerability gate stopped an update | `{container}`, `{details}` |
 | `docker.stack.failed` | deploying a stack failed, was rolled back or was stopped by its vulnerability gate, or an action on a Compose project failed | `{stack}`, `{details}` |
 | `docker.backup.failed` | a restic backup run failed | `{backup}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.container.crashed` | a container exited with an error (any code but 0, 130, 137, 143) | `{containers}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.container.restart_loop` | a container exited 3 or more times since the previous monitoring sample | `{containers}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.container.oom` | the kernel killed a process in a container for running out of memory | `{containers}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.container.unhealthy` | a container's health check turned unhealthy | `{containers}`, `{details}` |
+
+The four container events come from `docker events` read with every
+monitoring sample, from where the previous sample stopped by the
+machine's own clock (`Machine.docker_events_since`; at most the last hour
+and 200 events), so nothing is missed or sent twice. One notification per
+kind and sample lists every container. The first sample after the
+upgrade only records where to start - nothing from before is reported.
+Exit codes 137 and 143 are what `docker stop` leaves, so stopping or
+updating a container is not a crash; an OOM kill (137) is reported as
+such
+([`app/services/container_events.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/services/container_events.py)).
 
